1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:15,366
Great Idea #1 of CS61C: abstraction. A computer system is built as a stack of layers, from top to bottom.

2
00:00:15,366 --> 00:00:19,133
At the top is a high-level language, such as C.

3
00:00:19,133 --> 00:00:22,766
A compiler translates it into assembly language…

4
00:00:22,766 --> 00:00:29,166
…and an assembler turns that into machine code: 1s and 0s the machine can read directly.

5
00:00:29,166 --> 00:00:36,366
Below that is hardware. First, the architecture as block diagrams: say, two registers feeding an adder.

6
00:00:36,366 --> 00:00:44,066
Zoom into the adder and you find logic gates: adding two bits, an XOR gate gives the sum and an AND gate the carry.

7
00:00:44,066 --> 00:00:49,066
Zoom into one gate: it's built from transistors. That's the bottom layer.

8
00:00:49,066 --> 00:00:55,866
Between neighboring layers is a well-defined interface: follow it, and you needn't care about the details below.

9
00:00:55,866 --> 00:01:03,233
This series focuses on the interface between software and hardware: the instruction set architecture (ISA).

10
00:01:03,233 --> 00:01:15,133
An ISA specifies the assembly instructions, how they're encoded in bits, and architectural features such as registers, data types, memory addressing and I/O.

11
00:01:15,133 --> 00:01:23,533
Programs and compilers are written to the ISA as a standard; CPUs and other hardware are designed to that same standard.

12
00:01:23,533 --> 00:01:31,666
So a program can run on any CPU that follows the same ISA. The ISA this series studies is RISC-V.

13
00:01:31,666 --> 00:01:37,533
Assembly is mostly generated by compilers; few people write it by hand. So why learn it?

14
00:01:37,533 --> 00:01:45,200
A 2004 Slashdot post even claimed that knowing assembly is what separates mediocre programmers from excellent ones.

15
00:01:45,200 --> 00:01:55,100
Knowing assembly means knowing how the computer executes instructions, so even in a high-level language you write faster, leaner, cheaper programs.

16
00:01:55,100 --> 00:02:01,500
Before learning an ISA, let's look at the basic layout of a computer: the von Neumann architecture.

17
00:02:01,500 --> 00:02:08,166
The processor (CPU) does the computing. It has two parts: the control unit and the datapath.

18
00:02:08,166 --> 00:02:14,533
The datapath's main parts are the registers and the arithmetic logic unit (ALU), which does the math.

19
00:02:14,533 --> 00:02:23,666
Outside it are memory, which holds both program and data, and input/output (I/O) devices like keyboards and displays.

20
00:02:23,666 --> 00:02:30,000
Control, datapath, memory, input, output: the five components of a computer.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,866
The processor sends an address to read or write memory; an "enable" signal keeps a read from changing memory.

22
00:02:38,866 --> 00:02:46,433
Processors are fast: at 4 GHz, one clock cycle lasts just 0.25 nanoseconds.

23
00:02:46,433 --> 00:02:56,866
How short is that? Light travels about 300,000 km per second, yet in 0.25 ns it covers only 7.5 cm.

24
00:02:56,866 --> 00:03:07,700
Even data just 10 cm away takes light about 0.3 ns to reach: more than a cycle. So data must sit close to the processor.

25
00:03:07,700 --> 00:03:16,900
So every modern computer has at least two kinds of hardware for data. First, registers: inside the processor, small, but lightning fast.

26
00:03:16,900 --> 00:03:26,600
Second, memory: outside the processor and much larger, but one access takes about 100 ns, or 400 cycles.

27
00:03:26,600 --> 00:03:38,033
Now capacity: 32 registers × 4 bytes is just 128 bytes, while a laptop has 2 to 64 GB of memory, and a server up to 1 TB.

28
00:03:38,033 --> 00:03:49,466
Draw 128 bytes as a square. At this scale, 64 GB has about 500 million times the area: the square shrinks below a pixel.

29
00:03:49,466 --> 00:03:58,333
Main memory is usually DRAM on a separate chip (DDR3/4/5, HBM): many gigabytes for a few tens of dollars.

30
00:03:58,333 --> 00:04:06,266
But physics dictates that smaller is faster: registers are roughly 50 to 500 times faster than DRAM.

31
00:04:06,266 --> 00:04:13,266
Borrow Jim Gray's analogy: if getting data from a register is like recalling something in your head, taking one minute…

32
00:04:13,266 --> 00:04:19,733
…then memory, 100 times slower, is like driving to Sacramento to fetch a piece of paper you forgot.

33
00:04:19,733 --> 00:04:26,933
At 500 times slower, you'd drive to Los Angeles and back, all for a single piece of data!

34
00:04:26,933 --> 00:04:40,653
Registers are few, share precious chip area with the processor core, and are very expensive. So designing an ISA is a tango: compute in registers, and visit memory and disk sparingly.

35
00:04:40,653 --> 00:04:49,853
How should an ISA be designed? In the '70s and '80s, instructions grew ever more complex: one could read memory, compute and write back.

36
00:04:49,853 --> 00:05:00,886
Programs got shorter, maybe with fewer memory accesses, but the hardware was complex and costly. Such designs were later dubbed CISC: Complex Instruction Set Computers.

37
00:05:00,886 --> 00:05:09,420
In the early '80s, John Cocke at IBM designed the IBM 801: the first Reduced Instruction Set Computer (RISC).

38
00:05:09,420 --> 00:05:19,453
The opposite idea: keep the instruction set small and simple, and let software and compilers build complex operations from simple instructions.

39
00:05:19,453 --> 00:05:27,386
Programs need more instructions, but simple hardware runs far more of them per second, so they still finish sooner.

40
00:05:27,386 --> 00:05:35,486
Dave Patterson at Berkeley and John Hennessy at Stanford took the idea all the way, building the RISC and MIPS projects side by side.

41
00:05:35,486 --> 00:05:43,753
All three pioneers went on to win the Turing Award: Cocke in 1987, Patterson and Hennessy in 2017.

42
00:05:43,753 --> 00:05:54,986
Today RISC and CISC both thrive and keep evolving: many Windows PCs run Intel's x86, while many phones and Apple's own chips use ARM.

43
00:05:54,986 --> 00:06:03,353
A course has to pick an ISA. x86 is very complex, while old or made-up ISAs lack real compilers and software.

44
00:06:03,353 --> 00:06:14,453
RISC-V was born in 2010 in UC Berkeley's Par Lab, started by Patterson and Krste Asanovic. Around 2020, even MIPS switched over to it.

45
00:06:14,453 --> 00:06:24,553
It's popular for two reasons: it's open source and license-free. Anyone can use it for free, for teaching, research or commercial products.

46
00:06:24,553 --> 00:06:34,886
Academia and industry worldwide back RISC-V, and it has been used to build everything from embedded microcontrollers to warehouse-scale supercomputers.

47
00:06:34,886 --> 00:06:49,453
RISC-V comes in 32-, 64- and 128-bit variants. We'll study RV32I, the 32-bit base integer set; extras like multiplication live in extensions such as M.

48
00:06:49,453 --> 00:06:58,286
The whole architecture fits on a single page, the "Green Card", named after the famous IBM 360 green card of the 1960s.

49
00:06:58,286 --> 00:07:08,420
Simple and elegant, it's ideal for learning assembly and for designing computers. That's why CS61C teaches it. Go Bears!

50
00:07:08,420 --> 00:07:17,386
The series has 14 episodes. Next time, we start from one line of C, see how it becomes assembly, and meet RISC-V's registers.

